    content: "# Example Clues and Lists\n\n*Grade 6 English Language Arts · Unit 1 of 16 · Vocabulary Power · Lesson 4*\n\n## Objective\n\n**I can** infer word meaning from examples and illustrative lists.\n\n**Teacher focus:** Students explain *why* a choice works (a word, a sentence, a piece of evidence), not only label it. Prefer complete sentences on exit tickets.\n\n## Warm-up (3–5 minutes)\n\nCircle any unfamiliar word in this sentence and guess its meaning from neighbors: “The ranger’s concise warning — short, clear, and urgent — kept hikers from cutting the muddy switchback.”\n\nTurn and talk: Which earlier habit from Unit 1 might help today?\n\n## Teach\n\n### Big idea\n\nStrong readers do not stop at “I don’t know that word.” They hunt for **context clues** and **word parts** (roots, prefixes, suffixes). Today’s skill — **Example Clues and Lists** — builds that detective habit.\n\n### Why it matters\n\nAt Prosper Prep, clear reading and writing support scholarship habits: follow directions, cite evidence, and revise until a stranger can follow your thinking. Families use these skills when reading news, contracts, team emails, and faith or community texts. East Texas students deserve elite literacy tools — not tricks.\n\n### Language bank\n\n- **Context clue** = hints in the same sentence or nearby sentences.\n- **Root / affix** = word parts that carry meaning across many words.\n- **Connotation** = the feeling a word carries beyond its dictionary sense.\n\n### Worked example A\n\nImagine a practice for the spring showcase. Related practice numbers/labels you may see in items: set 11, set 0.\n\n**Mentor passage:**\n\n> Maya paused at the mailbox. The envelope was thin, but her name looked official. She did not open it on the porch. Inside, she set it on the kitchen table and washed her hands first, as if cleanliness could calm the shaking in her fingers. When she finally slid a thumb under the flap, she found three sentences and a deadline. Hope, she realized, could be heavy.\n\n**Step 1 — Restate the task** in your own words (main idea? craft? integration?).\n**Step 2 — Annotate:** underline claims, circle key words, star evidence.\n**Step 3 — Answer with a complete sentence** that includes a pointer to the text.\n**Step 4 — Check:** Could a partner find your evidence in under 10 seconds?\n\n**Sample response quality:** “The central idea is that careful hikers protect trails by slowing down at muddy markers, which is supported by the detail that most injuries happen when people rush the last half mile.”\n\n### Worked example B (different structure)\n\nKeep the skill of “Example Clues and Lists,” but switch the context to a scholarship essay workshop. Use a fresh short example (numbers 2 and 7 if you invent a list or ranking).\n\n1. Write a one-sentence goal.\n2. Show the markings (parts of speech, clues, or annotations).\n3. Produce the answer or revision.\n4. Add a concluding sentence that names the skill.\n\nTeachers: freeze after step 2 in live sessions so students cannot hide behind premature answers.\n\n### Common mistakes\n\n- **Watch for:** quoting a line without explaining how it supports the claim.\n- **Also watch for:** copying a whole paragraph as “evidence” instead of selecting the precise line, or fixing grammar by making sentences longer and muddier.\n- **Repair move:** Name the broken step in one sentence, then revise only from that step.\n\n### Connect to prior learning\n\nVocabulary and key-idea reading feed each other: you cannot summarize well if key words stay foggy.\n\n## Guided practice (we do)\n\n1. **Guided 1 —** Using the mentor passage above, apply “Example Clues and Lists.” Underline evidence.\n   - *Teacher/self-check note:* Answer must point to a specific line or phrase.\n\n2. **Guided 2 —** Partner challenge: write one wrong answer that looks tempting, then explain the trap.\n   - *Teacher/self-check note:* Naming traps builds metacognition.\n\n3. **Error hunt:** A fictional student wrote only “Because it says so.” What follow-up question should you ask before accepting that response?\n\n## Independent practice\n\nComplete each item with visible thinking (annotations, labels, or revisions).\n\n1. Problem 1: Infer or unpack a word related to today’s lesson using context or word parts. Invent a school-appropriate sentence with a clue (contrast, definition, or example). Mark the clue.\n2. Problem 2: Infer or unpack a word related to today’s lesson using context or word parts. Invent a school-appropriate sentence with a clue (contrast, definition, or example). Mark the clue.\n3. Problem 3: Infer or unpack a word related to today’s lesson using context or word parts. Invent a school-appropriate sentence with a clue (contrast, definition, or example). Mark the clue.\n4. Problem 4: Infer or unpack a word related to today’s lesson using context or word parts. Invent a school-appropriate sentence with a clue (contrast, definition, or example). Mark the clue.\n5. Problem 5: Infer or unpack a word related to today’s lesson using context or word parts. Invent a school-appropriate sentence with a clue (contrast, definition, or example). Mark the clue.\n6. Problem 6: Infer or unpack a word related to today’s lesson using context or word parts. Invent a school-appropriate sentence with a clue (contrast, definition, or example). Mark the clue.\n\n<details>\n<summary>Answer key (teacher / self-check — try first before expanding)</summary>\n\n1. Plausible meaning + identified clue type or root/affix; sentence is grammatical.\n2. Plausible meaning + identified clue type or root/affix; sentence is grammatical.\n3. Plausible meaning + identified clue type or root/affix; sentence is grammatical.\n4. Plausible meaning + identified clue type or root/affix; sentence is grammatical.\n5. Plausible meaning + identified clue type or root/affix; sentence is grammatical.\n6. Plausible meaning + identified clue type or root/affix; sentence is grammatical.\n\n</details>\n\n## Exit ticket\n\n1. In one sentence, what does “Example Clues and Lists” help a reader or writer do?\n2. Name the common mistake to avoid today (hint: related to quoting a line without explaining how…).\n3. Create one new practice item (with answer) a classmate could try in 2 minutes.\n\n*(Scored exit items also appear as multiple-choice checks below the lesson in Prosper Prep.)*\n\n## Stretch (optional)\n\nWrite a short CER paragraph (4–6 sentences) about the mentor passage that depends on “Example Clues and Lists.” Label C / E / R in the margin. Then invent a harder follow-up question for a classmate.\n",
