    content: "# Commas in a Series and Introductory Elements\n\n*Grade 6 English Language Arts · Unit 14 of 16 · Grammar: Punctuation and Capitalization · Lesson 2*\n\n## Objective\n\n**I can** place commas for lists and openers.\n\n**Teacher focus:** Students explain *why* a choice works (a word, a sentence, a piece of evidence), not only label it. Prefer complete sentences on exit tickets.\n\n## Warm-up (3–5 minutes)\n\nIn one minute, write two sentences about a Prosper Prep morning advisory. Underline every word that might be doing the job taught in “Commas in a Series and Introductory Elements.”\n\nTurn and talk: Which earlier habit from Unit 13 might help today?\n\n## Teach\n\n### Big idea\n\nGrammar is a clarity toolkit. Today’s focus — **Commas in a Series and Introductory Elements** — helps readers trust your meaning. In Grade 6 we practice noticing patterns, naming them precisely, and revising for a real audience (a teacher, a scholarship reader, a teammate).\n\n### Why it matters\n\nAt Prosper Prep, clear reading and writing support scholarship habits: follow directions, cite evidence, and revise until a stranger can follow your thinking. Families use these skills when reading news, contracts, team emails, and faith or community texts. East Texas students deserve elite literacy tools — not tricks.\n\n### Language bank\n\n- **Name the job** of a word (noun, pronoun, verb, modifier) before you argue about taste.\n- **Agreement** means forms match in number and person.\n- **Revision** beats guessing: change one broken piece, then re-read aloud.\n\n### Worked example A\n\nImagine a Prosper Prep morning advisory. Related practice numbers/labels you may see in items: set 8, set -3.\n\n**Mentor sentence:** “Because the evidence was incomplete, the jury asked for more time.”\n\n**Step 1 — Find the structure.** Dependent clause + comma + independent clause.\n**Step 2 — Apply today’s rule for “Commas in a Series and Introductory Elements.”** Mark the words doing that job.\n**Step 3 — Test a broken version.** What happens if we delete a key word or change agreement?\n**Step 4 — Revise a weak student sentence** so it follows the rule and still sounds natural.\n\n**Model talk:** “I checked the subject and the verb. They match. Then I read the sentence aloud to catch any leftover awkwardness.”\n\n### Worked example B (different structure)\n\nKeep the skill of “Commas in a Series and Introductory Elements,” but switch the context to an East Texas library quiet hour. Use a fresh short example (numbers 4 and 14 if you invent a list or ranking).\n\n1. Write a one-sentence goal.\n2. Show the markings (parts of speech, clues, or annotations).\n3. Produce the answer or revision.\n4. Add a concluding sentence that names the skill.\n\nTeachers: freeze after step 2 in live sessions so students cannot hide behind premature answers.\n\n### Common mistakes\n\n- **Watch for:** calling a topic (friendship) a theme or main idea.\n- **Also watch for:** copying a whole paragraph as “evidence” instead of selecting the precise line, or fixing grammar by making sentences longer and muddier.\n- **Repair move:** Name the broken step in one sentence, then revise only from that step.\n\n### Connect to prior learning\n\nConventions, word study, and style are the final polish that makes scholarship-ready writing readable.\n\n## Guided practice (we do)\n\n1. **Guided 1 —** Label or revise for “Commas in a Series and Introductory Elements” in: “The team of scholars present their CER paragraphs after advisory.” Show each step.\n   - *Teacher/self-check note:* Focus on the exact grammar job; explain the choice in a phrase.\n\n2. **Guided 2 —** Same skill, new sentence about a Prosper Prep morning advisory. Explain why your revision is clearer.\n   - *Teacher/self-check note:* Clarity + correctness beat ornamental wording.\n\n3. **Error hunt:** A fictional student wrote only “Because it says so.” What follow-up question should you ask before accepting that response?\n\n## Independent practice\n\nComplete each item with visible thinking (annotations, labels, or revisions).\n\n1. Problem 1: Revise or label for “Commas in a Series and Introductory Elements” in a sentence about a scholarship essay workshop (include a precise noun and a clear verb). Show the before/after if you revise.\n2. Problem 2: Revise or label for “Commas in a Series and Introductory Elements” in a sentence about a practice for the spring showcase (include a precise noun and a clear verb). Show the before/after if you revise.\n3. Problem 3: Revise or label for “Commas in a Series and Introductory Elements” in a sentence about a Prosper Prep morning advisory (include a precise noun and a clear verb). Show the before/after if you revise.\n4. Problem 4: Revise or label for “Commas in a Series and Introductory Elements” in a sentence about a Tyler-area nature walk journaling stop (include a precise noun and a clear verb). Show the before/after if you revise.\n5. Problem 5: Revise or label for “Commas in a Series and Introductory Elements” in a sentence about a Grade 6 book-club meeting (include a precise noun and a clear verb). Show the before/after if you revise.\n6. Problem 6: Revise or label for “Commas in a Series and Introductory Elements” in a sentence about a student newspaper deadline (include a precise noun and a clear verb). Show the before/after if you revise.\n\n<details>\n<summary>Answer key (teacher / self-check — try first before expanding)</summary>\n\n1. Correct application of Commas in a Series and Introductory Elements; before/after shows intentional change; explanation names the grammar job.\n2. Correct application of Commas in a Series and Introductory Elements; before/after shows intentional change; explanation names the grammar job.\n3. Correct application of Commas in a Series and Introductory Elements; before/after shows intentional change; explanation names the grammar job.\n4. Correct application of Commas in a Series and Introductory Elements; before/after shows intentional change; explanation names the grammar job.\n5. Correct application of Commas in a Series and Introductory Elements; before/after shows intentional change; explanation names the grammar job.\n6. Correct application of Commas in a Series and Introductory Elements; before/after shows intentional change; explanation names the grammar job.\n\n</details>\n\n## Exit ticket\n\n1. In one sentence, what does “Commas in a Series and Introductory Elements” help a reader or writer do?\n2. Name the common mistake to avoid today (hint: related to calling a topic (friendship) a theme…).\n3. Create one new practice item (with answer) a classmate could try in 2 minutes.\n\n*(Scored exit items also appear as multiple-choice checks below the lesson in Prosper Prep.)*\n\n## Stretch (optional)\n\nFind a paragraph in your own writing (or invent 6–8 sentences about a Prosper Prep morning advisory). Annotate today’s grammar target, revise two places, and write a 3-sentence reflection on how clarity improved.\n",
